.TH MKPART 8
.SH NAME
mkpart \- add partitions after the existing ones
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBmkpart\fR [\fB\-n \fIsectors\fR] \fIdevice type\fR[\fB/4\fR]\fB:\fIsize\fR[\fB@end\fR] ...
\fBmkpart \-l\fR \fIdevice\fR
\fBmkpart \-s\fR \fIdevice\fR
\fBmkpart\fR [\fB\-n \fIsectors\fR] \fB\-g \fIN device\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Mkpart
keeps the partitions already in the MBR of
.I device
(the root file system the system runs from) and fills the following
empty slots with new partitions, one per argument.
Each starts on a 4 MiB boundary after the one before it, the usual
erase unit of an SD card (Galtzagorriak MINIX, spec R12.32).
It never moves an existing partition.
The kernel reads the new table at the next boot.
.PP
.I type
is hex: 81 for MINIX, 82 for swap.
.I size
is in MB,
.IB N %
for that share of the space that is left, or
.B *
for all that is left.
.B @end
puts the partition at the end of the card (on a 4 MiB boundary); the
arguments after it fill the space before it.
The MBR slots follow the order of the arguments, whatever the place on
the card.
A swap partition (82) stays below 4 GiB \- 1 MiB (a
.B %
or
.B *
size is cut to that); a MINIX partition may be larger (a MINIX V3
.BR /home ,
up to 2 TiB).
.PP
.B 82/4
(spec R12.34, cards of 32 GB or more) makes a MINIX partition (81) that
holds up to four swap subpartitions (82) of 4 GiB \- 1 MiB each:
.B /dev/hd3a
to
.B /dev/hd3d
when it is the third.
They start 4 MiB into it, 4 GiB apart; its first sector holds their
table.
Its size is cut to the whole subpartitions that fit.
Swap goes to the first one; MM finds the others itself (see
.BR swapon (8)).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-l
List the partition table: slot, type, start and size in sectors, size in MB.
.TP
.B \-s
Print the size of
.I device
in MB.
.TP
.BI \-g " N"
Make partition
.I N
reach up to the next partition after it, or the end of the card.
.B "setup \-H"
uses it to give a MINIX V3
.B /home
the space that swap had on a card made before.
.TP
.BI \-n " sectors"
A dry run: as if
.I device
had that many sectors.
It prints the new table and writes nothing.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP 30
.B "mkpart /dev/hd0 81:2048 82:64 81:*"
/usr (2 GB), swap (64 MB), /home (the rest): the first boot on a card
below 8 GB.
.TP
.B "mkpart /dev/hd0 81:2048 82:50%@end 81:*"
/usr, then swap at the end of the card (half of what is left after
/usr, at most 4 GiB \- 1 MiB), /home between them: the first boot on
an 8 GB or larger card (spec R12.34).
.TP
.B "mkpart -n 31116288 /dev/hd0 81:2048 82:50%@end 81:*"
The same, as a dry run for a 16 GB card.
.TP
.B "mkpart /dev/hd0 81:2048 82/4:*@end 81:*"
/usr, four 4 GiB swap subpartitions at the end of the card, /home
(all the space between them): the first boot on a card of 32 GB or
more.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mkfs (1),
.BR erase (8),
.BR part (8).
